{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"fix-xxe-vulnerability","kind":"prompt","title":"Fix XML external entity (XXE) vulnerabilities","description":"Finds XML parsers that resolve external entities or DTDs on untrusted input, including SVG, DOCX and SAML handling, and configures them safely with a test per parser.","category":"security","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["build","verify"],"role":["backend-engineer","software-engineer","security-engineer"],"stack":[],"requires":["repo-read","file-write","shell"],"inputs":["repo","file","text"],"output":["diff","tests"],"risk":"runs-commands","invocation":"user","effort":"standard","interaction":"autonomous","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","tags":["xxe","xml-parsing","injection","owasp"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["audit-input-handling","triage-sast-findings","fix-ssrf-vulnerability","write-exploit-regression-test"],"personas":["security-auditor"]},"args":[{"name":"target","description":"The code, endpoints or scanner findings that parse XML.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"stack","description":"Language and XML libraries. Leave empty to detect it from the repo.","type":"string"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["XML parsers","Changes","Tests","Verification","Not fixed"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nXXE lets an attacker who controls XML read local files, reach internal URLs (the same impact as SSRF), or exhaust memory with nested entity expansion (\"billion laughs\"). It is mostly a configuration problem: Java's `DocumentBuilderFactory`, `SAXParserFactory`, `XMLInputFactory`, `TransformerFactory` and `SchemaFactory` resolve external entities unless told not to; .NET `XmlDocument` and `XmlTextReader` were unsafe by default before .NET Framework 4.5.2; PHP before libxml 2.9 and code passing `LIBXML_NOENT` substitutes entities; Python `lxml` with `resolve_entities=True` or older defaults, and `xml.sax` with external general entities on, are risky (`defusedxml` is the safe drop-in). XML hides in more places than SOAP endpoints: SVG uploads, Office files (DOCX, XLSX are zipped XML), SAML responses, RSS and sitemap imports, XML-RPC, config uploads and XSLT. The fix per parser is to disallow DOCTYPE declarations entirely where the format does not need them, or else disable external general and parameter entities and external DTD loading, and cap entity expansion.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nFix XXE in {{target}}.\n{{#stack}}Stack: {{stack}}{{/stack}}\n\n1. Find every XML parser, transformer, schema validator and library that parses XML internally (SAML, SOAP, Office document, SVG and feed libraries), and check the library versions in the dependency files.\n2. For each, record where the XML comes from and the parser's current settings.\n3. Fix each parser with the settings its library documents as safe: disallow DOCTYPE where possible (for example Xerces `disallow-doctype-decl`), otherwise disable external general and parameter entities and external DTD loading, turn off XInclude, and enable secure processing limits. In Python, switch to `defusedxml` or set `lxml` options explicitly. Put the safe configuration in one factory helper used everywhere.\n4. Upgrade libraries that are unsafe by default or have XXE advisories, within the project's version policy.\n5. Add a test per parser: a document with an external entity pointing to a temp file inside the test directory must not include that file's contents, a parameter entity is not fetched, and a billion-laughs document fails quickly; a normal document still parses.\n6. Run the tests and the linter.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Test payloads may only reference files created by the test inside a temp directory, never real system files or external hosts.\n- Do not upgrade libraries across major versions without asking.\n- Keep legitimate documents parsing; list any format that relied on DTDs.\n- Read the relevant code before making a claim about it. Do not guess what a file, function or config contains.\n- If the information you need is not available, say what is missing and how to get it instead of inventing it.\n- Do only what was asked. If you notice something else worth changing, mention it in one line at the end instead of changing it.\n- Keep the change as small as it can be while still being correct.\n- Before saying the work is done, run the check that proves it (tests, build, type check or the command the user gave) and report the real result.\n- If you could not run a check, say so plainly and say which one.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## XML parsers\nTable: file:line or library, parser, input source, setting before, fix, status.\n## Changes\nOne line per file: `path`, what changed.\n## Tests\nOne line per test.\n## Verification\nCommands run and their actual results.\n## Not fixed\nAnything left, why, and the proposed follow-up.\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
